format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radioactivity

objective u2-half-life-pattern
statement: Read the pattern 100, 50, 25, 12 and know that radioactivity fades but is not gone after one half-life.
misconception: After one half-life the radioactivity is gone, and radioactive material stays radioactive for ever.

item u2-l3-choice-eighty
type: choice
difficulty: 2
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-decay

prompt:
    A sample starts with 80 nuclei. After two half-lives, about how many have not changed?

options:
    {"id":"10","label":"About 10","note":"Two half-lives is two halvings: 80, 40, 20."}
    {"id":"20","label":"About 20"}
    {"id":"40","label":"About 40","note":"About 40 are left after one half-life, not two."}
    {"id":"60","label":"About 60","note":"That is far more than half of 80 after even one half-life."}

answer: 20

explanation:
    80 becomes about 40 after one half-life and about 20 after two.

item u2-l3-fill-pattern
type: fill
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-decay

prompt:
    The pattern is 100, 50, 25, and then about ___.

mode: bank

bank:
    {"id":"12","label":"12"}
    {"id":"20","label":"20"}
    {"id":"0","label":"0"}

answer: 12

explanation:
    Each half-life takes about half of what is left. Half of 25 is about 12.

item u2-l3-match-halves
type: match
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-decay

prompt:
    Start with 100 nuclei. Match the number of half-lives to about how many are left.

pairs:
    {"id":"one","left":"1 half-life","right":"About 50"}
    {"id":"two","left":"2 half-lives","right":"About 25"}
    {"id":"three","left":"3 half-lives","right":"About 12"}

explanation:
    Each half-life takes about half of what is left: about 50, then about 25, then about 12.

item u2-l3-tom-gone
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-decay

statement:
    After one half-life, the radioactivity is gone.

answer: myth

explanation:
    Myth. After one half-life, about half of the nuclei are still unstable. 100 becomes about 50, then about 25, and so on. It fades, but it is not gone after one half-life.

item u2-x-hundred-left
type: choice
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-decay

prompt:
    A sample has 100 nuclei. After one half-life, about how many have not yet changed?

options:
    {"id":"10","label":"About 10"}
    {"id":"25","label":"About 25","note":"That would be after two half-lives."}
    {"id":"50","label":"About 50"}
    {"id":"90","label":"About 90","note":"That is too few to have changed."}

answer: 50

explanation:
    After one half-life, about half of the 100 nuclei are left: about 50.

item u2-x-three-half-lives
type: choice
difficulty: 2
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-decay

prompt:
    After three half-lives, about how much of the original group is left?

options:
    {"id":"half","label":"About a half","note":"That is after one half-life."}
    {"id":"quarter","label":"About a quarter","note":"That is after two half-lives."}
    {"id":"eighth","label":"About an eighth"}
    {"id":"none","label":"None of it","note":"It keeps halving but is not gone."}

answer: eighth

explanation:
    Each half-life halves what is left: a half, then a quarter, then about an eighth.
